## Bugs to fix

- [ ] **DAP "port error" on the second laptop.** Starting the debugger errors out. Cause is
      almost certainly a legacy `type = "executable"` codelldb adapter there — nvim-dap only
      substitutes `${port}` for `type = "server"`, so codelldb receives the literal `${port}`.
      Fix: run `scripts/setup-nvim-dap.sh` (step 3 now moves such files aside), `:Lazy sync`,
      restart. Checks + error capture: `notes/23-debug-lazyvim-troubleshoot.md`. The primary
      machine is verified clean.
- [x] **`tokenize_string` overran the source on unterminated input** — fixed 2026-11-27: the
      increment is inside the `closing_quote` branch, so the NUL exit leaves the cursor alone.
- [ ] **Tokenizer still silently drops truly unknown chars** (`@`, lone `!`, …). Punctuation,
      operators and digits are now real tokens; the stopgap `default:` remains for the rest.
      Part 7 of `current.md` owns the error contract. `notes/41` defect 7.
- [ ] **`tokenize` ignores `list_push`'s return value** (the word push *and* the `*` push). A
      grow failure goes unnoticed. defect 2.
- [ ] **`grow_list_capisity` returns `void`**; failure surfaces only through the sticky
      `had_error` side effect. defect 3.
- [ ] **`had_error` is overloaded + sticky** — cannot distinguish "malformed SQL" from "out of
      memory". defect 4.
- [ ] **`token_type_name` is not implemented** (header declaration commented out). The 27-case
      body is recoverable from `tests/scratch.c` (`scratch_type_name`).
      `notes/42-token-type-name.md`.
- [x] **EOF token fields fixed 2026-11-27:** `token_string = lexer.input_string +
      lexer.cursor_position`, `token_length = 0`, `source_index = lexer.cursor_position`;
      test-first assertion in `select_input_is_select_then_eof`. defect 6 closed.

## Next up — tokenizer (in order)

- [x] **Parts 1-5 DONE:** keyword table (11 entries, case-insensitive, prefix-safe),
      punctuation and operators (giant `switch`, 2-char lookahead for `<= >= !=`), identifier
      spelling (`isalpha || isdigit || '_'` after byte 1), numbers (`else if (isdigit)`,
      `tokenize_number` → `TOKEN_NUMBER`). Commits `bbd234a`, `fb348ee`, `af7c1c6`, `1adf1fb`.
- [x] **Part 6 — strings DONE 2026-11-27:** quote-excluded span, interior whitespace, empty
      string, trailing bytes, unterminated error — all green. `current.md` Part 6 has the spans.
- [x] **Part 7 — errors DONE 2026-11-27:** partial list + `had_error`, stderr line at detection,
      no EOF on error, `error_pos` preserved. `SELECT@` → 1 token/pos 6, `@SELECT` → 0,
      `SELECT 42 @ ;` → 2 tokens/pos 10, unterminated → 0 tokens/pos 0. Still open separately:
      OOM path must use the same reporting contract (no offending byte to index for `%c`).
- [~] **Part 8 — EOF/list robustness:** EOF pointer at end DONE 2026-11-27; remaining:
      `token_list_free(NULL)` test, 10,000-token stress under ASan, OOM-path reasoning.
- [ ] **Part 9 — `token_type_name`** (see bug above; recover from `tests/scratch.c`).
- [ ] **Part 10 — full checkpoint:** `"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 3;"` (10 exact spans in
      `current.md`) + grammar-free `"FROM SELECT , , ;"`.
- [ ] **Guide checklist tests** (~line 2610): position checks; prefix cases
      `"selection"`/`"SEL"`/`"SE"`/`"S"` → `TOKEN_IDENT`; `"SELECT@"` → error naming the
      character; identifiers with digits/underscores (`user_id2`) but not starting with a digit;
      `token_list_free(NULL)` safe; 10,000-token stress input.

## Open design decisions (decide before the parser grows)

- [x] **OOM/error reporting — DECIDED 2026-11-26:** return the partial list with
      `had_error = true`, `error_pos` at the bad byte, `error_msg` set; caller frees. Print
      one line to stderr at the detection site; truncate the list at the bad byte with **no
      EOF** (EOF is success-only). One contract for lexical errors and allocation failure.
      The existing `errorrrr`/`return NULL` tail must be rewritten.
- [ ] **`token_type_name` spelling:** enum names (`"STAR"`, `"LTE"`) vs user-facing SQL symbols
      (`"*"`, `"<="`). The guide derives `expect()` messages from it, so decide now.
- [ ] **EOF token field convention** (see bug above).

